[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186344834 :answer-field/correct 17592186343227 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186262664 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨2,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =c⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨2c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4-2t=2c \\\\ 3{t}^{2}=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2-t.$ Substituting in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & =2-t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+t-2 & =0 \\\\ (3t-2)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨2,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=-1,\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨4(-1)-(-1)^{2},(-1)^{3}⟩=⟨-5,-1⟩ \\\\ r(\\frac{2}{3}) & =⟨4(\\frac{2}{3})-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2},{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}⟩=⟨\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨2,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27}).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-4ac98174-23fb-56c7-aa42-87bd53136ffc" :activity/id #uuid "4ac98174-23fb-56c7-aa42-87bd53136ffc" :activity/title "Tangent Vectors and Tangent Lines to Curves" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048377 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-ccf8c39a-29e3-592d-8a48-1ca1d41e9f28" "step-9feee794-e3e7-5c76-b0a7-b75fa9c75d71" "step-3ffa00d6-c195-5c51-ba40-87eda1859e72" "step-9a79291f-1a57-5bc2-94db-68fc1780e073" "step-feabdf5c-47